{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>I will go down with you to Egypt.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): He promised him that He would be with him in that descent, and because of this He said ארד עמך and did not say אורידך, just as He said אעלך. <b>And I will also surely bring you up.</b> He hinted to him that he would be buried in the land of Israel, which is higher than all lands, where the gate of heaven is. And He added <b>also bring you up</b>, for from there he would ascend in another, supernal ascent to shine in the light of life — and this is the doubling of the ascent." "<b>And furthermore,</b> it is possible to interpret the doubling of the ascent as a promise to his seed, that He would redeem them after the exile and bring them up to the land of Israel; and after they are settled in the land of Israel, where the essential fulfillment of Torah and commandments is, He promised them yet another ascent, and that is the World to Come hidden for his seed. This alludes to what the Sages, of blessed memory, said: all Israel have a portion in the World to Come. And it is the matter of Balaam, the adversary of Israel, who admitted against his will and saw this in prophecy, for he said (Numbers 23:10): ״תמות נפשי מות ישרים ותהי אחריתי כמוהו״. He hinted that among all the nations there are no inheritors of the Garden of Eden except Israel, and on this he immediately based himself (ibid.): ״הן עם לבדד ישכן״. And this is why He used the expression אנכי, for in the merit of the Torah that his seed was destined to receive, the Shekhinah would be with them in the exile of Egypt, and they would merit those two ascents, in which there is benefit for body and soul in both worlds." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: <b>I will go down with you to Egypt</b> — an allusion to the Shekhinah, which went down with them to Egypt. This is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: ״When they were exiled to Egypt, the Shekhinah was with them.״ And in order to allude to this, Onkelos translated אנא אחות, and did not translate אנא אתגלי, as in the manner of (Exodus 34) וירד ה׳, which he translated ואתגלי ה׳. And since he everywhere intends to distance corporeality and flees from mentioning any word indicating it, he should have translated here too אנא אתגלי — but he translated אנא אחות, because he wished to allude to this saying: ״When they were exiled to Egypt, the Shekhinah was with them.״ And on this account he used for it the language of descent literally, even though it is a word indicating corporeality. And the fact that Scripture says מצרימה with the addition of the letter heh, and likewise עלה with the letter heh, is an allusion to the Shekhinah. Therefore the heh is inscribed both in the descent and in the ascent, for it went down with Jacob to Egypt and it is what ascends with them — and it is the final heh of the Name. And it is what sustained and provided for all the land of Egypt during the five years of famine. Thus Joseph hinted in his words, when he said (Genesis 47:23–24): ״הא לכם זרע וזרעתם את האדמה״, and it is written: ״ונתתם חמישית״. And it is what destroyed the Egyptians in Egypt and at the sea. And it is called יד (״hand״), and it is said of Egypt in the fifth plague, as it says (Exodus 9:3): ״הנה יד ה׳ הויה״. And He mentioned five species: in horses, in asses, in camels, in cattle, and in sheep. And it is said of the sea: ״וירא ישראל את היד הגדולה״. And it is what the prophet Ezekiel mentioned at the time of wrath, in the destruction of the Temple, for it is written (Ezekiel 16:43): ״וגם אני הא דרכך בראש נתתי נאם ה' אלהים״. And He already mentioned five times לא אחמול — that is, in the passage (Ezekiel 5) ״לכן אבות יאכלו בנים בתוכך״, until the passage ״ואראה על הרקיע״, you will find five times ״לא תחוס עיני ולא אחמול״. And Daniel (2) likewise mentioned it, saying: ״הא כדי פרזלא לא מתערער בחסף טינא״, and he mentioned five species: דהבא, כספא, נחשא, פרזלא, חספא. And behold, it is found in Scripture three times — in the Torah, in the Prophets, and in the Writings. And so it is found in the Masorah: heh three times in reading — הא לכם זרע, וגם אני הא, הא כדי פרזלא. And this heh is with Israel in all the exiles. This is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: ״When they were exiled to Egypt, the Shekhinah was with them,״ as it says: אנכי ארד עמך מצרימה — and this is the expression עמך, from the root צרה (trouble), alluding to what is written (Psalms 91:15): ״עמו אנכי בצרה״. ״When they were exiled to Babylon, the Shekhinah was with them,״ as it says (Isaiah 43): ״למענכם שלחתי בבלה״. ״When they were exiled to Edom, the Shekhinah was with them,״ as it says (ibid. 63): ״מי זה בא מאדום״, etc. Set it upon your heart who calls this זה. And so this attribute is mentioned again in its vestment, and it is written (Exodus 15:2): ״זה אלי ואנוהו״. You learn thereby that this heh is alluded to and inscribed in all the exiles — Egypt, Babylon, and Edom. And this is why the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: אל תירא מרדה מצרימה — He did not say אל תירא לרדת, in the manner of what is written (Judges 7:10): ״ואם ירא אתה לרדת״. And likewise Joseph said to his father (Genesis 45:9): ״רדה אלי אל תעמוד״, and did not say רד אלי, to hint that this heh went down with him. And since this heh went down with Jacob and the seventy souls to Egypt, you need not wonder at the abundance of blessing in their seed, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 1:7): ״ובני ישראל פרו וישרצו וירבו ויעצמו במאד מאד״ — because the Shekhinah was with them and was in their midst, and there YHWH ordained the blessing until they increased to sixty myriads, among whom the Torah was given and the Shekhinah appeared to them." "<b>And now,</b> you should understand the saying of the sage who used to expound in public, who would say: ״A certain woman was in Egypt who gave birth to sixty myriads in one womb״ — for the matter of birth is the bestowal of power. And the reason he needed to expound this saying in public — and it is a saying that astonishes one who does not understand the intent — is that the people were dozing, and the sage wished to rouse them from their slumber with a single saying indicating hyperbole and exaggeration, so that all the listeners from the crowd would be astonished by it, even though it was simple and clear, not astonishing, to the individuals and the sages of the same caliber. And when they awoke from their slumber, those who did not understand needed to ask him who she was; then he answered them according to their grasp, according to the plain sense of the saying: this is Jochebed, who gave birth to Moses, who was equivalent to sixty myriads. And the reason he used the expression אשה is the expression that Solomon, peace be upon him, mentioned in his wisdom (Proverbs 31:10): ״אשת חיל מי ימצא״. And it says (ibid. v. 12): ״אשת חיל עטרת בעלה וכרקב בעצמותיו מבישה״ — and understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:02.123593102Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}